@@s037 Exceptions when asked
@@s038 "Unless explicitly asked for": in some exercises the text asks precisely for an exception. Example: <code>tail</code>, <code>drop</code> and <code>init</code> of week 2 must throw <code>NoSuchElementException</code>. There, it must be done.
@@s039 The style scale (slide 11, week 2)
@@s040 It is the grid the course uses to judge the code. Each row goes from left (worse) to right (better). <i>"Always choose the best possible style for an exercise and your abilities."</i>
@@s041 <span class="t-no">Condemned</span> (failed)
@@s042 <span class="t-ora">Forgivable</span> (average grade)
@@s043 <span class="t-ok">Enlightened</span> (high grade)
@@s044 variables
@@s045 values
@@s046 assignments
@@s047 value bindings (<code>val</code>)
@@s048 <code>return</code>
@@s049 value of the expression
@@s050 type <code>Any</code> / <code>Object</code>
@@s051 parametric polymorphism
@@s052 loops
@@s053 tail recursion &lt; simple recursion &lt; fold
@@s054 composing dedicated higher-order functions
@@s055 <code>if</code> conditions &lt; pattern matching
@@s056 using the dedicated API
@@s057 exceptions
@@s058 Option or Either monad
@@s059 Footnote to the slide: the rule holds "unless asked for explicitly, or really important for memory use". For example, tail recursion is used if it really is needed to avoid exhausting the stack.
@@s060 The same function, going up the scale: sum of a list
@@s061 <span class="t-no">Condemned.</span> Variable, loop, assignment, return: four prohibitions in five lines. It compiles and works, but it fails.
@@s062 <span class="t-ora">Forgivable, low.</span> Tail recursion with an accumulator: no variables, but a lot of code for a standard thing.
@@s063 <span class="t-ora">Forgivable.</span> Simple recursion with pattern matching: more readable, but it rewrites by hand a pattern that already exists.
@@s064 <span class="t-ora">Forgivable, high.</span> The fold names the pattern: only what is specific remains, the 0 and the +.
@@s065 <span class="t-ok">Enlightened.</span> A dedicated API exists: use it. (In the exercises, only if it is among the allowed functions.)
@@s066 An error, going up the scale: mean of a list
@@s067 <span class="t-no">Condemned.</span> Exception: the type does not say that it can fail.
@@s068 <span class="t-ok">Enlightened.</span> The failure is a value. Whoever uses <code>mean</code> chains it with <code>map</code>/<code>flatMap</code>.
@@s069 From week 2: explicit recursion "smells"
@@s070 From the week 2 exercise sheet
@@s071 "From now on, the use of explicit recursion is bad-smell for us. Only use explicit recursion when dealing with a non-standard iteration. Otherwise, you should use a suitable HOF. Similarly, you should only use fold if any of the other simpler HOFs cannot."
@@s072 Order of choice: <b>dedicated API</b> (<code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>exists</code>…) → <b>fold</b> → <b>recursion</b> only if the iteration is unusual.
@@s073 Constraints of the individual exercises
@@s074 Many exercises add a specific prohibition. At the exam expect questions with similar instructions: read them before writing.
@@s075 Exercise
@@s076 Constraint
@@s077 Why
@@s078 S1 · isSorted
@@s079 no pattern matching, tail recursion with <code>@tailrec</code>
@@s080 imitating a <code>for</code> over the indices
@@s081 S1 · compose
@@s082 no <code>Function1.compose</code> / <code>andThen</code>
@@s083 write it yourself
@@s084 S2 · all
@@s085 only the functions at the top of <code>Exercises.scala</code> and those of the earlier exercises
@@s086 the course list is not the library one
@@s087 S2 · foldLeft with foldRight
@@s088 no <code>reverse</code>
@@s089 reverse is a case of foldLeft
@@s090 S3 · map, flatMap… on Option
@@s091 <b>with</b> pattern matching, as methods
@@s092 so that you can avoid it later
@@s093 S3 · headGrade, map2, sequence
@@s094 no pattern matching
@@s095 use map, flatMap, for
@@s096 S3 · variance
@@s097 no pattern matching, no <code>map</code> on Option, avoid <code>isEmpty</code>
@@s098 use flatMap
@@s099 S4 · toList, take, drop, takeWhile, forAll
@@s100 <b>with</b> pattern matching
@@s101 S4 · map, filter, append, flatMap
@@s102 with <code>foldRight</code>, no recursion
@@s103 When the compiler fails you by itself
@@s104 The course's <code>project.scala</code> compiles with <code>-Werror</code>: <b>every warning becomes an error</b>. So the file does not compile, and a homework that does not compile does not pass.
@@s105 Warning
@@s106 Typical cause
@@s107 <code>match may not be exhaustive</code>
@@s108 a case is missing, or all the cases have an <code>if</code> guard
@@s109 <code>Unreachable case</code>
@@s110 a general case before a specific one
@@s111 <code>Unreachable case except for null</code>
@@s112 a <code>case _</code> after cases that already cover everything
@@s113 Complete messages and solutions: <a href="errori.html">Compiler errors</a>.
@@s114 Academic honesty
@@s115 During the course you <b>may</b> discuss the solutions, consult AI, paste pieces of code on the Discord.
@@s116 You <b>may not</b> hand in solutions written by others, AI included, nor copy substantial parts. "You must type your hand-in yourself."
@@s117 <b>At the exam</b>: no consulting other people, no AI of any kind.
